# Working agreement

Read `AGENT.md` for engineering invariants, then `docs/implementation/STATUS.md`
and `docs/implementation/requirements.json` before changing code.

This is a Neovim presentation host. Web browsing is one provider. Keep Neovim
state authoritative and use explicit window/grid identities. Providers are
trusted installed code; their documents and extraction results are untrusted.

## Session protocol

1. Inspect `git status --short` and preserve unrelated edits. Do not reset the tree.
2. Run `npm run harness` to see incomplete requirements and the next milestone.
3. Read the relevant ADR and acceptance cases in `docs/implementation/PLAN.md`.
4. Implement one coherent milestone, including its integration and error paths.
5. For implementation milestones, run `npm run check`. For guest, focus, text lifecycle, or permission changes,
   also run `npm run smoke:electron` on a desktop (Linux: `xvfb-run -a`). Inspect
   `.local/review/presentation.png` and exercise affected interactive workflows.
6. Update STATUS with exact checks, failures, known limits, and the next step.
   Update requirements evidence only after verification. Passing infrastructure
   checks does not mean browser safety parity or all requirements are complete.
7. Keep future work in implementation docs, current behavior in architecture
   docs, and durable decisions in `docs/decisions/`.

Do not silently enable shell expansion for provider commands, expose Neovim IPC
to documents, disable TLS verification, reuse Chrome's live profile directory,
or claim browser security equivalence without the release-gate evidence.

No publishing or remote repository creation is needed for local implementation.
Package directories must remain extractable into independent repositories.

## Testing delegation and cost

This is a standing request to delegate substantial routine verification to a
sub-agent. Keep architecture, implementation decisions, difficult debugging and
final acceptance with the primary agent.

- Use one test agent, explicitly selecting `gpt-6-luna` with `low` reasoning.
  Give it a fresh context (`fork_turns: "none"` where supported) and a short,
  self-contained brief: repository path, changed behavior/files, exact commands,
  runtime environment, expected artifacts and known diagnostic messages. On this
  machine, Node 24 is selected with `PATH=/opt/homebrew/opt/node@24/bin:$PATH`.
- Delegate a full check suite, desktop/package smoke, or a substantial log-triage
  job. Run a single quick focused command directly when delegation overhead would
  exceed its value. Do not spawn an agent merely to wait for one command.
- The test agent runs only the assigned checks, sequentially, and does not edit
  source, tests, fixtures, dependencies or implementation evidence. It may write
  test logs to `.local/review/`. No recursive delegation. It reads this agreement
  and relevant invariants; it need not reread the entire roadmap for execution-only
  work. It must not interpret this section as a request to spawn another agent.
- Freeze the code being verified. Do not edit it, run another build, or launch a
  competing desktop workflow while verification is running. Independent planning
  or reading may continue. The primary agent does not repeat the same checks just
  to confirm the test agent's report.
- Return a concise report: commands and exit codes, failure location and a short
  relevant excerpt, log/artifact paths, and any checks not run. Inspect full logs
  only where a failure needs investigation. Never equate an infrastructure pass
  with requirement completion.
- Stop on an unexpected failure and report it to the primary agent. No speculative
  fixes or blind reruns. The primary diagnoses the cause and assigns the next
  focused check; at most one targeted rerun for a specifically identified transient
  condition before escalating again.
- If the requested model/delegation is unavailable, state that briefly and execute
  the assigned checks directly. Do not silently spawn a more expensive model.

Use focused checks while implementing, then the required suite once at the coherent
milestone boundary. A new change, failure or unresolved concern may justify another
run. Package smoke is required when packaging, shipped runtime/resources, preload,
Electron security configuration or package-specific behavior changes; otherwise run
it at a browser integration/release checkpoint rather than every small slice.
Keep required desktop and interactive verification for affected behavior. Delegate
interactive work only if the child has the needed tools; otherwise the primary does
it. Do not copy and modify an entire manual smoke harness for routine verification;
prefer a reusable review mode and escalate missing harness support.

For instructions/documentation-only changes, use `npm run harness`, diff review and
`git diff --check`; do not run build, desktop or packaged suites unless the changed
content affects their execution. Record this scope accurately in the handoff.

## Project scope

Non-browser provider implementations belong in their own repositories and separate
sessions. The existing Markdown test package is retained as API conformance evidence;
do not expand it here. Current implementation work focuses on host/core and browser
functionality. A jusi-neovim plugin-authoring skill is deferred until explicitly requested.
